      "latency_ms": 2226.6,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 504,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and one of the most abundant and essential substances on Earth. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\n* **Formula:** $H_2O$\n* **Composition:** Each molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n* **State of Matter:** Water is famous for being the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states: \n  * **Liquid** (the water we drink, rivers, oceans)\n  * **Solid** (ice, snow, glaciers)\n  * **Gas** (water vapor, steam, humidity)\n\n### 2. Physical and Chemical Properties\nWater has several unique and unusual properties that make it vital for life:\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. Because of this, it easily carries nutrients, chemicals, and minerals wherever it goes.\n* **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some insects to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water move up the roots of plants).\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the Earth and the internal temperature of living organisms.\n* **Expansion When Freezing:** Unlike most substances, water expands and becomes less dense when it turns to solid ice. This is why ice floats, which prevents lakes and oceans from freezing solid from the bottom up, allowing aquatic life to survive the winter.\n\n### 3. The Ecological and Biological View\n* **The Driver of Life:** Water is the primary component of all known living cells. The human body, for example, is made of about 60% water. Without it, biochemical reactions required to sustain life cannot occur.\n* **Earth's Blanket:** About 71% of the Earth's surface is covered in water, mostly in oceans. It shapes the planet’s geography, drives weather patterns, and makes Earth habitable.\n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It is the lifeblood of our planet, the cradle of biology, and the stage upon which Earth's weather and geology play out."
